  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nOur goal is to find a sequence of non-negative numbers that minimizes the evaluation function \\(2n \\cdot \\text{max}(\\text{conv}(a, a)) / (\\text{sum}(a))^2\\), where \\(n\\) is the sequence length. To achieve this, we use a multi-stage optimization strategy that combines global search with local refinement. We start by initializing candidates from different distribution forms (linear, exponential, power, sigmoid, etc.), then optimize each using the Adam algorithm in the logit space transformed by their respective basis functions. Finally, we polish the best candidate using coordinate descent to explore promising regions.\n\nMathematical specification:\nThe sequence is generated from a basis matrix using coefficients. For fixed length \\(n\\), we define:\n1. Mixed basis: \\(B_{mix}(t) = \\{\\cos(2\\pi m t), \\sin(2\\pi m t)\\}_{m=1}^{M}\\)\n2. Polynomial basis: \\(B_{poly}(u) = \\{u^k\\}_{k=0}^{K}\\)\n3. Sigmoid basis: Specific transitions defined by \\(\\eta\\) and center.\n\nThe decision vector \\(\\theta\\) represents these coefficients. The evaluation function uses exact convolution through FFT, and normalization occurs via `full_convolve_fft` with defensive clipping.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Validate and normalize the parent sequence.\n2. Initialize candidates by generating profiles from each deterministic family for lengths 128 and 256.\n3. Optimize each candidate using Adam through `soft_max_adam`.\n4. Document and update the best candidate upon improvement.\n5. Extend best candidate to 512 using interpolation and optimize further.\n6. Apply coordinate descent polishing to the top candidates.\n7. Select the best candidate for final score evaluation.\n8. Return the sequence or fallback if time runs out.\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Optimization lengths: 128, 256, 512\n- Fallback: If no improvement after time limits, use optimized linear profile at 256\n- Variations: Linear, exponential, power, sigmoid, cubic, quadratic mixtures\n- Phases separated by time limits and \u03c1-level quality bounds\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\nWe budget:\n- 300 seconds for Adam optimizations at each distinct length\n- 500 seconds for interpolation and longer Adam\n- 300 seconds for coordinate descent polishing\n- 300 seconds for final selection and fallback\n\nIf evaluations are invalid or convergence is poor, we employ fallback mechanisms. We compute objective scores safely and avoid premature convergence by restarting adaptive processes.\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Evaluate candidate through exact `full_convolve_fft`.\n2. Ensure non-negativity via `normalize_nonneg`.\n3. The best candidate is the one with lowest score.\n4. If runtime exceeds, return current best candidate.\n5. Else, fall back to linear profile.",
